well let's get more now from ibrahim  he's a senior adviser to  two and spokesman for president erdogan  and joins us live  from the presidential palace in istanbul  thank you so much for joining us  here on al jazeera just explain to us  more why does turkey not want a  diplomatic solution to this crisis  no we support the diplomatic solution  and we call on the men's group actually  to come up with a  timetable with a new proposal that is  actionable  that has specific guidelines to  end the current round of  hostilities but also more importantly to  find a way to end the  occupation of azeri lens by armenia that  has been the root cause of this problem  the men's group which was established  almost 30 years ago has done very little  in fact to  bring an end to this frozen conflict in  the southern caucasus at its time  really to come up with a new proposal  new road map that's what the azeri's  have been demanding that's what we've  been supporting  yes to assist fire by the ceasefire must  be sustainable and the only way to make  it sustainable  is also to talk about ending the  armenian occupation of azeri lens  as you know uh karabakh nagorno-karabakh  is  is the land of the azerbaijan state of  azerbaijan according to u.n decisions  according to numbers  of number of resolutions osc decision  etc  so yes to a diplomatic solution but that  that needs to come up with  or combined with a a timetable with the  roadmap that will bring an end to the  occupation of nagorno-karabakh by  armenia so do you support then the  efforts of the french russian and u.s  representatives who are heading to  geneva to start  thrashing out a road map  if they're calling only for a cisfar  working just on a ceasefire it will be  nothing more than a repeat of what  went on over the last 30 years or so uh  it  it is almost certain to fail if it  doesn't  also involve a detailed uh  plan to end the occupation and  yes we want this clashes to come to an  end we don't want any civilians or  others to be hurt  in this conflict uh too many people have  already lost their lives uh more than a  million azeri  people had to flee nagorno-karabakh in  the 1990s when armenia occupied  this part of azerbaijan yes we want an  end to all of this human suffering the  fighting  this conflict in the southern caucasus  but if the miss group is to be credible  and if they're going to claim any  political and moral authority at this  point to bring an end to this conflict  they must also start  a serious work on ending the occupation  and  what's turkey's role here why exactly is  is turkey taking such a a strong  position in this particular conflict  well for two reasons southern uh  caucuses uh you know what happens there  of course is of direct concern to us  uh it affects uh you know our borders it  affects our uh  uh our region uh our relationship with  azerbaijan with georgia and the larger  caucasus region that's number one number  two we have a very special relationship  with azerbaijan you know we call  ourselves one nation two states  and we have a long-standing uh military  agreement  with azerbaijan in addition to many  other cultural economic political  diplomatic  agreements with azerbaijan um and we  would like to see  an end to this occupation of azeri lens  you keep saying this as an occupation  about this cause we believe  i'm sorry to cut you off there you keep  saying this is an occupation but this  isn't these people  these people have been living there this  goes this predates 1990  uh for predates the the the the late 80s  these people have been living their  generations have been living there  decades the schools you could argue this  goes back to the  the soviet days in the ussr  i think you're referring to the armenian  population of nagorno-karabakh  that's a fact that they've been living  there together with the azeries for for  many decades under the soviet republic  rule that is true  and the azares have always said that you  know once nagorno-karabakh is liberated  those armenians who live there will live  under uh you know just like you know any  other uh  uh citizens they will be entitled to  their rights uh they will have their  freedom of religion  culture identity and and so on so forth  yes it is occupation according to all  the young resolutions just like the  israeli occupation of the palestinian  lands there is no legal or political  debate about that it's a fact  so so is turkey going to stay involved  in this  until the armenians  leave the region of nagorno-karabakh  until  armenians pull out completely pull all  their citizens who've been living there  for decades is that  your dividing line turkey stays engaged  in this fight until that happens  no no one has called for civilian  armenians to leave nagorno-karabakh  if once uh in fact when uh once  nagorno-karabakh is liberated and  becomes part of azerbaijan again those  who  live there armenian citizens i'm uh i  mean  of course they will be allowed they will  have the rights to stay there because  they've been living there for many years  as i said in the past before the  armenian occupation the azeries and  armenians were living together  but let's not forget the fact that more  than a million azeri's had to flee  nagorno-karabakh after uh the the  armenian occupation and about uh  as far as remember about 30 plus  thousand almost 40 000 people died over  the last 30 years we do not want to see  a repeat  just want to get indeed how deeply  turkey is involved in this because there  have been allegations that turkey is  sending troops at sending syrians via  turkey  into this fight supplying arms to the  azeries  it appears that turkey is very very  invested in this  at a time when you're already um  fighting with nato members and disputes  with nato members in the eastern  mediterranean  getting involved in libya now it appears  there is a third front there  happening in the caucasus i mean are you  worried that turkey  is spreading itself a bit too thin  fighting on too many fronts  well first of all there is no evidence  of the so-called syrian fighters going  fighters going to uh  to azerbaijan or facilitated by us but  there  there is evidence of lebanese armenian  uh individuals i guess or militia  you know going there i mean they're  posing before cameras and saying calling  openly  you know for a holy war or something  again well the french president and the  syrian president  say that's not nonsense  the french president the syrian  president have said there are syrians  being created  can you take  they are not good references really when  it comes to facts uh either the  syrian president or the french president  i'm not sure because we've had to deal  with them in the past  on many different occasions uh but i  know the french position you know they  oppose  uh turkey almost on every single issue  of  strategic significance in syria in libya  and we believe  france is on the on the wrong side of  history i mean in  in syria france supports members of pkk  which is a terrorist organization listed  as a terrorist organization  in the eu in france and other places uh  in libya france supports haftar  who is supported by russia and france is  a nato member i don't know how it  reconciles  this with uh you know with being a  member of nato and  talking about the uh the security and  safety of the southern flank of uh  of nato in in north africa uh etc i mean  they're all  complicated issues uh there but you know  our main concern  is to find a diplomatic solution to this  but as i said  for this piece to be sustainable or  ceasefire to be sustainable it needs to  be  based on a roadmap a plan to end the  occupation  that that will be the only way to solve  this problem so that we don't have to  repeat this for the next 30 years  okay ibrahim khalid i'm afraid we are  out of time but thank you so much  for joining us from the presidential  palace in istanbul abraham kaleen  a senior adviser to the turkish  president recep tay pardon thank you

